READ THE FILE, NOT THE REPO. THE AUDIT'S LICENCE COLUMN WAS WRONG TWICE.

The plan for this task listed four generators to bundle, with a licence for each. Those licences
came from the repository LICENSE file (and agree with GitHub's licence detector). On downloading
the actual .scad files, TWO of the four carried a DIFFERENT licence in their own header — and in
both cases the header was the more restrictive one. One of them was NonCommercial.

A repo-level LICENSE file is not the licence of every file under it. The author's notice inside the
work is a specific statement about that work, and where the two disagree the specific one cannot be
waved away. This is the licensing spelling of the same lesson the render test encodes: a check that
looks clean at one level ("the repo is MIT") is not the check you needed ("what does the thing we
actually ship say?"). Open the file.

  DROPPED — universal_stand (IOIO72/scad-universal-stand @ 78446ad)
    Plan said: MIT, and the repo's LICENSE file IS a stock MIT (Copyright (c) 2022 Tamio Honma).
    The .scad file itself says, in its header:
        "This work is licensed under the Creative Commons
         Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 4.0 International License"
    NonCommercial AND ShareAlike — the exact pair spec §2 was written about, behind an
    MIT-looking repo. The README has NO licence section, so nothing corroborates the MIT file.
    The header is still there at HEAD; the .scad has exactly one commit ever ("Add files via
    upload", 2022-04-19), so it was never a stale notice the author later corrected.
    NC cannot be absorbed under any reading — Tinkerex is sold and this image is redistributed —
    and the conflict is not ours to resolve. Not bundled. Precedent: the Screw Cap rejection
    below. Unverified provenance is not a licence, and CONTRADICTED provenance is worse.
    It remains available as a bring-your-own file, which carries no redistribution exposure.

  KEPT WITH A RECORDED CONFLICT — vase_box (DrLex0 @ 5dbc4c9)
    The .scad header says "Released under Creative Commons - Attribution - Share Alike license".
    Everything else in the repo says CC BY 4.0, specifically and deliberately:
      - LICENSE.md is the COMPLETE CC BY 4.0 International legal text.
      - README.md: "### License [Creative Commons - Attribution](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/)"
      - GitHub's licence detector reads the repo as CC-BY-4.0.
    The README also records that this began life on Thingiverse in 2019 and was migrated to
    GitHub in 2023-05, and the CC BY 4.0 text arrived in that migration commit. A stale
    Thingiverse-era "-SA" in a header comment is the ordinary explanation, and the operative
    grant is the licence text the author committed.
    Bundled as CC-BY-4.0 on that evidence. Flagged here rather than quietly normalised, because
    the artifact we redistribute does contain a line contradicting the licence we claim for it,
    and whoever reviews this should get to see that and overrule if they disagree.

