MODIFICATIONS

  None. Every bundled file is byte-for-byte its upstream at the pinned commit.

  hex_cell_holder was expected to need its 4x8 default lowered to 3x3: it takes 54 s under
  OpenSCAD 2021.01/CGAL, which the sidecar's 60 s cap would not survive once a user raised
  anything. Measured in THIS image, which builds against Manifold, it renders its UPSTREAM
  4x8 default in 329 ms — about 160x faster, and 45x inside the 15 s budget. Left unmodified
  because the measurement said so rather than because the estimate did. Re-measure before
  assuming this still holds if the image's OpenSCAD or its geometry backend changes.

  Cold render at defaults, measured in this image (amd64 under emulation on an arm64 host, so
  production native amd64 will be at least this fast):

    vase_box           223 ms     2,109 bytes      24 verts /     40 tris
    hex_cell_holder    329 ms   256,785 bytes   9,937 verts / 20,162 tris
    washer             257 ms    32,732 bytes   1,280 verts /  2,560 tris
    probe_plate        248 ms    21,126 bytes     776 verts /  1,596 tris

  vase_box's 2,109 bytes sits only ~109 bytes above the 2,000-byte floor that
  test_every_entry_renders_a_substantial_model_at_its_defaults asserts. That is NOT a stub: a
  vase-mode box is a single-wall open shell, so 40 triangles is the right answer, and the
  rendered bounding box (53.4 x 66.2 x 70.6 mm) matches its declared 50 x 25 x 70 defaults
  with the lid laid out beside the box. Verified by reading the mesh out of the 3MF, not by
  trusting the byte count. Recorded because the margin is thin: if that assertion ever goes
  red on vase_box, check the geometry before assuming the file broke.

